            <ArticleTitle>Depressive Symptoms among Plastic Surgery Residents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e1516</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/GOX.0000000000001516</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Background" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">A literature review did not reveal any study investigating the prevalence of depression among plastic surgery residents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Methods" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We conducted a cross-sectional mail survey and included all Saudi Plastic Surgery residents in our local program in Saudi Arabia. The Beck Depression Inventory II was used.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Results" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The inventory showed that mild, moderate, and severe depressive symptoms were prevalent in 20.6%, 38.2%, and 11.8%, respectively. Junior residents were more prone to depression.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Conclusions" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The high prevalence rate of depression among plastic surgery residents is alarming, and further studies are warranted. The problem has received no attention in the plastic surgery literature.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
